                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Curse of the Old Gods is a short point and click mystery adventure that feels like it was made as a love letter to old detective games. I play as FBI Agent Moreton after a nightwatchman is brutally killed at a museum in New York. What starts as a fairly normal investigation quickly turns into something much stranger involving ancient cults, strange artefacts and the Old Gods. I like that the game does not take long to get into the main mystery because there is always something suspicious going on in the background.\n\nThe investigation is the main reason I enjoyed it. I have to explore different locations, examine objects, talk to people and work out how the different clues fit together. There are also puzzles that make me pay attention to the things I find rather than simply clicking through every conversation. I like this style of adventure because I am given enough information to make progress without the game turning into a huge complicated RPG. It is mostly about looking carefully and thinking about what I have already discovered.\n\nThe Lovecraftian setting works well too. The game takes place in a modern world, but there is clearly something much older and more dangerous underneath the murder investigation. The museum setting gives the story a good starting point because the exhibition about African cults immediately gives me a reason to start asking questions. I also like how the mystery gradually becomes more serious instead of explaining everything straight away. It gives the short adventure a nice feeling of uncovering something that was supposed to stay hidden.\n\nThe visual style is probably going to be the biggest deciding factor for me. The game deliberately uses digitised images presented in a very old EGA style with a limited colour palette. It looks rough by modern standards, but I think that is part of the charm. The old computer style works surprisingly well with the mystery and horror atmosphere, especially when combined with the General MIDI soundtrack. It makes the game feel like a forgotten adventure from another era rather than another modern indie game trying to copy the same visual style.\n\nThe biggest weakness is the length. The developer describes the average session as around an hour, and that is definitely something I notice when I reach the end. I like what is here, but the story and investigation are over before the game has enough time to become something much bigger. Some of the puzzles can also be a little awkward because I sometimes have to work out exactly what the game expects me to interact with. There is a walkthrough available if I get completely stuck, but I would rather figure things out naturally, and a few moments made that harder than they needed to be.\n\nI also think the simple presentation means the story has to carry most of the experience. There is no combat system or large progression system distracting me from the investigation, which I actually prefer, but it also means every puzzle and conversation matters. When the mystery is working, I enjoy the slower pace and the strange atmosphere. When I get stuck on a small interaction, there is not much else happening to keep me interested. Still, I appreciate that the game stays focused instead of adding unnecessary mechanics just to make the experience longer.\n\nOverall, Curse of the Old Gods is a short but enjoyable mystery adventure that I think does a good job with its old school point and click style. I like the murder investigation, Lovecraftian story, strange museum setting and deliberately retro presentation, and the puzzles give me enough to think about without becoming overwhelming. The very short length and occasional awkward puzzle design hold it back from being one of my higher rated adventure games, but the fact that it is completely free makes it much easier to recommend. For me, it is worth playing when I want a quick mystery with a creepy atmosphere and a very old fashioned adventure game feel.\n\nRating: 7.4/10\n",
